<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyprus Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> Convention was a secret agreement signed on 4 June 1878 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Britain undertook to defend the Ottoman lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and in return the sultan allowed it to occupy and administer Cyprus while keeping formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Britain annexed the island outright in 1914 when the Ottomans entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s side.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the mid-1870s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and other European powers sought to stop Russian expansion into lands held by a weakening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> pushed west and south toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the Dardanelles. British control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> was meant to forestall that advance, and the agreement, reached in secret negotiations earlier in 1878, was tied to British support for the Ottomans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a>. Britain had been offered the island three times before, in 1833, 1841 and 1845, before accepting it from Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>By the convention the sultan consented to assign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to be occupied and administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, while Ottoman rights over the territory were retained. The excess of the island's revenue over the cost of its government was to be paid to the sultan as an annual fixed payment, which let the Porte maintain that it had only handed over administration for a time; the arrangement was sometimes described as a lease. The sum was fixed at exactly 92,799 pounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a>, eleven shillings and three pence, a calculation Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-storrs">Ronald Storrs</a> later likened to faked accounts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Cyprus Tribute was never paid to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>: it went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a> to service Ottoman Crimean War loans on which they had defaulted, angering both the Porte and the Cypriots, who also bore the costs of the colonial administration. Agitation against it was constant. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, then Undersecretary of State for the Colonies, criticised it in 1907, Parliament voted a permanent annual grant of 50,000 pounds and reduced the tribute accordingly.</p><p>From the start the British met Greek Cypriot calls for enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, voiced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Kition in a speech at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larnaca">Larnaca</a> welcoming the first High Commissioner. The island became a military base on the sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and a new harbour at Famagusta, completed in 1906, raised its importance near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. On 5 November 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> formally annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a>, ending the convention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyprus Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Cyprus Convention</b> of 1878, the Ottoman Empire allowed Britain to occupy and administer Cyprus, while keeping formal sovereignty, in return for British support against Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cyprus">British Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
